185
00:10:01,680 --> 00:10:05,580
No one knows more about it than the folks here

186
00:10:05,680 --> 00:10:08,660
at McElhenney Company.

187
00:10:08,760 --> 00:10:12,320
Sequestered on Avery Island in Louisiana's coastal wetlands,

188
00:10:12,420 --> 00:10:15,560
it's the only manufacturer of authentic Tabasco sauce.

189
00:10:18,600 --> 00:10:21,700
Each year, four production lines turn out close to two

190
00:10:21,701 --> 00:10:26,700
bottles of the world's most popular hot sauce.

191
00:10:26,800 --> 00:10:28,940
Well, Tabasco is really everywhere.

192
00:10:29,040 --> 00:10:31,740
And when I mean everywhere, I mean it is everywhere.

193
00:10:31,840 --> 00:10:35,240
We bottle in 20 different languages and dialects.

194
00:10:35,340 --> 00:10:38,080
We export to 160 plus countries.

195
00:10:40,660 --> 00:10:43,260
The sauce's creator, Edmund McElhinney,

196
00:10:43,360 --> 00:10:47,120
was a Louisiana banker who went broke following the United

197
00:10:47,220 --> 00:10:49,200
States Civil War.

198
00:10:49,201 --> 00:10:53,940
In 1868, he stumbled into the sauce making business.

199
00:10:54,040 --> 00:10:57,240
He actually made it for the family table first.

200
00:10:57,340 --> 00:11:01,040
And then friends and family said, you know, this is pretty good.

201
00:11:01,140 --> 00:11:02,440
Why don't you sell it?

202
00:11:02,540 --> 00:11:06,220
He started off small and just got bigger and bigger.

203
00:11:06,320 --> 00:11:08,780
For five generations, the Mecklehennie family

204
00:11:08,880 --> 00:11:11,620
continued the tradition with the same simple recipe.

205
00:11:13,720 --> 00:11:19,200
Tabasco pepper, salt, and vinegar.

206
00:11:19,201 --> 00:11:23,200
Most of McElhaney's Tabasco peppers are grown in Central

207
00:11:23,300 --> 00:11:27,200
and South America and shipped to the factory.

208
00:11:27,300 --> 00:11:32,740
Trucks arrive loaded down with 5,000 gallons of pepper mash.

209
00:11:32,840 --> 00:11:36,220
The mash is made of ground up peppers, which is the seeds,

210
00:11:36,320 --> 00:11:39,760
the skins, the placenta, the entire part of the, all of the

211
00:11:39,860 --> 00:11:42,660
pepper just ground up.

212
00:11:42,760 --> 00:11:46,620
Workers pump the mash into 50 gallon oak barrels, once used

213
00:11:46,621 --> 00:11:48,120
used in bourbon distilleries.

214
00:11:52,120 --> 00:11:54,860
Next, the barrels are sealed, and a layer of salt

215
00:11:54,960 --> 00:11:55,960
is added on top.

216
00:11:58,760 --> 00:12:00,660
We put the salt on the top of the barrels.

217
00:12:00,760 --> 00:12:03,500
When the fermentation starts, it actually pushes juices up,

218
00:12:03,600 --> 00:12:06,100
and the wet juice gets into the salt.

219
00:12:06,200 --> 00:12:08,040
Then you have a liquid on top of the barrel.

220
00:12:08,140 --> 00:12:10,140
After it dries, it acts like a seal.

221
00:12:10,240 --> 00:12:13,940
It keeps the air from going back in.

222
00:12:13,941 --> 00:12:18,120
McElhinney stores 60,000 barrels in a huge warehouse

223
00:12:18,220 --> 00:12:20,180
and leaves them to age for three long years.

224
00:12:23,720 --> 00:12:27,780
And we still are not quite sure what goes on during that aging

225
00:12:27,880 --> 00:12:28,680
process.

226
00:12:28,780 --> 00:12:32,020
We really aren't sure about the chemical processes

227
00:12:32,120 --> 00:12:34,360
or actually what's happening to the mash.

228
00:12:34,460 --> 00:12:36,360
And it still remains a mystery to us.

229
00:12:38,760 --> 00:12:42,400
After the lengthy wait, the barrels are finally opened

230
00:12:42,401 --> 00:12:46,400
and the aged mash heads to the mixing plan

231
00:12:46,500 --> 00:12:48,940
for the final ingredient, vinegar.

232
00:12:52,840 --> 00:12:57,240
1,400 gallons of vinegar is mixed with 3,000 pounds of mash

233
00:12:57,340 --> 00:13:00,960
and blends for up to 28 days.

234
00:13:01,060 --> 00:13:04,360
Vinegar adds pungency, but just as important,

235
00:13:04,460 --> 00:13:07,360
it dilutes the capsaicin mixture.

236
00:13:07,460 --> 00:13:11,300
This scales down the heat level from 50 to 4,000

237
00:13:11,301 --> 00:13:15,800
Scoville units. 50,000 is too hot.

238
00:13:15,900 --> 00:13:17,300
No one likes 50,000.

239
00:13:17,400 --> 00:13:21,140
You know, you take it and it burns your mouth.

240
00:13:21,240 --> 00:13:24,140
One vat like this produces about 1,600 gallons

241
00:13:24,240 --> 00:13:26,840
of finished sauce.

242
00:13:26,940 --> 00:13:29,160
Four vats hold more than Edmund McElhenney brewed

243
00:13:29,260 --> 00:13:32,260
in his entire lifetime.

244
00:13:32,360 --> 00:13:35,300
The massive quantities create a problem.

245
00:13:35,400 --> 00:13:37,700
The combination of peppers, salt, and vinegar

246
00:13:37,800 --> 00:13:41,300
produce acidic fumes, making it difficult to absorb.

247
00:13:41,301 --> 00:13:43,400
to breathe.

248
00:13:43,401 --> 00:13:47,740
Surprisingly, most workers don't seem to notice.

249
00:13:47,741 --> 00:13:48,900
If you've been here long enough,

250
00:13:48,901 --> 00:13:50,940
you get used to that caustic environment.

251
00:13:50,941 --> 00:13:54,320
Been here a while, you stop coughing.

252
00:13:54,321 --> 00:13:55,340
I haven't been up here in a while,

253
00:13:55,341 --> 00:13:59,880
so it's catching me as well.

254
00:13:59,881 --> 00:14:04,320
Metal and machines are no match for Tabasco fuels.

255
00:14:04,321 --> 00:14:07,400
We have two forklifts, basically the same models,

256
00:14:07,401 --> 00:14:08,960
and this one's new.

257
00:14:08,961 --> 00:14:12,920
It hasn't been exposed to the harsh conditions of processing of Tabasco.

258
00:14:13,460 --> 00:14:15,300
This one has been exposed for three years.

259
00:14:15,820 --> 00:14:16,940
Everything is gone.

260
00:14:17,600 --> 00:14:20,280
So this one has about another six months or so left in it,

261
00:14:20,320 --> 00:14:21,560
and then we'll get rid of it.

262
00:14:24,620 --> 00:14:28,140
Tradition gives way to high technology during the packaging process.

