309
00:16:58,680 --> 00:17:02,680
Savoring the sizzle is nothing new.

310
00:17:02,681 --> 00:17:05,680
The chili plant grows wild in South and Central America.

311
00:17:09,180 --> 00:17:12,720
In 4000 BC, chili peppers were domesticated

312
00:17:12,820 --> 00:17:15,900
in what is now Ecuador and Peru.

313
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:19,800
By the seventh century AD, they had spread to the areas of Mexico

314
00:17:19,900 --> 00:17:22,500
and the Caribbean.

315
00:17:22,600 --> 00:17:27,200
Natives refer to the fiery fruit simply as a chili.

316
00:17:27,300 --> 00:17:30,600
Then when Columbus arrived in the Caribbean,

317
00:17:30,601 --> 00:17:35,100
described this pepper-like heat in the dishes served to him

318
00:17:35,200 --> 00:17:36,380
by the native peoples.

319
00:17:36,480 --> 00:17:40,380
And he called it aji, which is loosely translated

320
00:17:40,480 --> 00:17:43,880
to mean pimento or pepper in Spanish.

321
00:17:43,980 --> 00:17:49,960
And so we mistakenly now call chilies peppers.

322
00:17:50,060 --> 00:17:53,120
True chili peppers burn with an entirely different chemistry

323
00:17:53,220 --> 00:17:56,620
than the black peppers Columbus knew.

324
00:17:56,720 --> 00:17:59,600
Chili, the food dish, is short for the Spanish term

325
00:17:59,601 --> 00:18:04,300
term chili con carne, which means peppers with meat.

326
